ue Career**\\n\\nSpokas signed with Hapoel Tel Aviv in 1995, making his professional debut on September 2, 1995, in a match against Maccabi Haifa. [2] Over the course of his seven-year career in the Israeli Premier League, Spokas played for several clubs, including Hapoel Tel Aviv, Beitar Jerusalem, and Maccabi Petah Tikva. He earned a reputation as a skilled midfielder, known for his vision and passing ability. **Notable Achievements**\\n\\nDuring his time in the Israeli Premier League, Spokas helped Hapoel Tel Aviv win the Toto Cup in 1999. [3] He also earned several call-ups to the Israel national football team, although he never made an official appearance. **Post-Playing Career**\\n\\nAfter retiring from professional football in 2002, Spokas went on to coach youth teams in Israel and the United States. He currently resides in Tel Aviv, where he works as a football analyst for Israeli sports media outlets. **References**\\n\\n[1] \\\"Northeastern University Athletics - 1992 NCAA Tournament\\\". Northeastern University Athletics. Retrieved 2023-02-15. [2] \\\"Hapoel Tel Aviv - Maccabi Haifa, 2-1\\\". Israeli Football Association. 1995-09-02. Retrieved 2023-02-15. [3] \\\"Hapoel Tel Aviv Wins Toto Cup\\\". Haaretz. 1999-02-10. Retrieved 2023-02-15.\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [], \"prior_explanation\": \"Eric Spokas is not a widely known athlete, and there is no clear information on which league he plays in based on common knowledge.\", \"fallible_cards\": [], \"fallible_previous_answer\": [\"NCAA Division I men's basketball league\", \"Israeli Premier League\"]}"
